---
import BlockLayout from '../layouts/BlockLayout.astro';
import studio from '../data/buildworks.json';
import {calculatePilot, makerBrief, PILOT_DEFAULTS, INPUT_LIMITS} from '../lib/buildworks.mjs';
import '../styles/buildworks.css';
const description = 'An original El Segundo small-goods studio concept: pins, stickers, magnets, ornaments and elements. Explore designs, maker briefs and honest pilot economics.';
const pilot = calculatePilot(PILOT_DEFAULTS);
const money = (n:number) => new Intl.NumberFormat('en-US',{style:'currency',currency:'USD'}).format(n);
const fields = [
  {key:'quantity',label:'Units ordered',step:'1'}, {key:'unitCost',label:'Manufacturing / unit ($)',step:'0.01'},
  {key:'packaging',label:'Packaging / ordered unit ($)',step:'0.01'}, {key:'freight',label:'Inbound freight / batch ($)',step:'0.01'},
  {key:'tooling',label:'Tooling + samples / batch ($)',step:'0.01'}, {key:'rejectPercent',label:'Reject allowance (%)',step:'0.1'},
  {key:'price',label:'Direct sale price / unit ($)',step:'0.01'}, {key:'shipping',label:'Seller-paid shipping / sale ($)',step:'0.01'},
  {key:'feePercent',label:'Selling/payment fee (%)',step:'0.1'}, {key:'feeFixed',label:'Fixed fee / sale ($)',step:'0.01'},
  {key:'returnReserve',label:'Return reserve / sale ($)',step:'0.01'}, {key:'sellThrough',label:'Saleable units sold (%)',step:'0.1'},
  {key:'wholesalePrice',label:'Wholesale price / unit ($)',step:'0.01'}
];
const jsonLd = {'@context':'https://schema.org','@type':'CreativeWork',name:studio.name,url:studio.canonical,description:studio.disclosure,dateCreated:studio.date,creativeWorkStatus:'Concept design study',isAccessibleForFree:true};
---
<BlockLayout title="El Segundo Buildworks · small things, made thoughtful" description={description} themeColor="#efe9d4" isolated immersive showNetworkStrip={false} jsonLd={jsonLd} alternates={[{type:'application/json',href:'/buildworks.json',title:'Buildworks concept and research notebook'}]}>
<div class="bw" data-buildworks>
  <a class="bw-skip" href="#concepts">Skip to the concepts</a>
  <header class="bw-nav"><a href="/" class="bw-mark">POINTCAST ↗</a><span class="bw-stamp">El Segundo, California / studio concept 01</span><nav aria-label="Buildworks"><a href="#concepts">The collection</a><a href="#brief">Make a brief</a><a href="#business">The business</a><a href="#sources">Sources</a></nav></header>
  <section class="bw-hero" aria-labelledby="bw-title"><div><p class="bw-kicker">EL SEGUNDO BUILDWORKS</p><h1 id="bw-title">Small things.<br/><em>A sense<br/>of place.</em></h1><p class="bw-dek">Pins, stickers, magnets, ornaments and the little elements that bring them together. Art for a pocket, a wall, a gift, a day.</p><a class="bw-button" href="#concepts">Explore the six studies <span aria-hidden="true">↓</span></a><p class="bw-note" style="margin-top:20px">An independent studio idea, with the making questions left open.</p></div><figure class="bw-hero-art"><img src="/images/buildworks/bench.svg" alt="Six original flat design studies: a coral wave pin, cobalt coastal sticker, yellow tide magnet, green canopy ornament, sun medallion and graphic paper tags" width="1065" height="667" fetchpriority="high"/><figcaption><span>THE FIRST SIX / DRAWN FOR EL SEGUNDO</span><span>CONCEPTS, NOT INVENTORY</span></figcaption></figure></section>
  <aside class="bw-disclosure" aria-label="Studio status"><strong>A proposal you can work with.</strong><p>{studio.disclosure} The place influences the work; it does not imply city endorsement. The brief and calculator run in this page and send nothing to a maker.</p></aside>

  <section class="bw-section" id="concepts" aria-labelledby="concepts-title"><div class="bw-section-head"><div><p class="bw-kicker">01 / THE FIRST COLLECTION</p><h2 id="concepts-title">Coast. City.<br/><em>Working day.</em></h2></div><p>Wave sets, the marine layer, a street canopy, the geometry of work. Six visual prototypes turn local observations into a shared graphic language. Materials and sizes are proposals.</p></div><div class="bw-concepts">{studio.concepts.map(concept=><article class="bw-concept" id={concept.id}><div class="bw-concept-top"><span>BW / {concept.number}</span><span>ORIGINAL CONCEPT</span></div><img src={`/images/buildworks/${concept.id}.svg`} alt={`${concept.name}, an original flat ${concept.family.toLowerCase()} concept drawing`} width="510" height="440" loading="lazy"/><div class="bw-concept-copy"><p class="bw-kicker">{concept.family}</p><h3>{concept.name}</h3><p>{concept.story}</p><details><summary>Proposed material + proof questions</summary><p><strong>Scale.</strong> {concept.size}</p><p><strong>Material.</strong> {concept.material}</p><p><strong>First question.</strong> {concept.question}</p><p><strong>Sample check.</strong> {concept.qc}</p></details><button class="bw-button bw-button--light" type="button" disabled data-use-concept={concept.id}>Use in my brief <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></button></div></article>)}</div><p class="bw-note" style="margin-top:20px">Original vector illustrations, not product photographs, CAD or production-ready artwork. <a href="/images/buildworks/provenance.json">Read the provenance record ↗</a></p></section>

  <section class="bw-section" id="making" aria-labelledby="making-title"><div class="bw-section-head"><div><p class="bw-kicker">02 / FROM IDEA TO A SMALL RUN</p><h2 id="making-title">Earn the<br/><em>next batch.</em></h2></div><p>A shared production framework for small goods, paper components and a future puzzle line. A supplier’s public capability is a lead to investigate; a signed-off sample is a different kind of evidence.</p></div><div class="bw-flow"><article><p class="bw-kicker">01 / DEFINE</p><h3>One job.<br/>One audience.</h3><p>Write intended use, scale, budget ceiling and rights log. Make a full-size paper model; choose whether this needs to exist as a physical object.</p></article><article><p class="bw-kicker">02 / QUOTE + PROOF</p><h3>One controlled<br/>revision.</h3><p>Ask for comparable quantities and itemized costs. Freeze an artwork revision, inspect front/back proofs and approve a physical sample before authorizing a run.</p></article><article><p class="bw-kicker">03 / PILOT + INSPECT</p><h3>One small<br/>learning batch.</h3><p>Use an affordable quantity. Record the supplier lot, inspect every piece in the first pilot, track defects, test packaging and retain an approved reference sample.</p></article><article><p class="bw-kicker">04 / REVIEW</p><h3>One honest<br/>decision.</h3><p>Compare cost, demand evidence, returns and material waste with the brief. Reorder, revise, repair or stop. A bigger batch needs a reason beyond a cheaper unit price.</p></article></div><div class="bw-notice"><strong>No fabrication recipe here.</strong> This notebook covers design, sourcing and inspection. Metal finishing, machining, chemical processes and powered cutting belong with qualified makers operating appropriate facilities.</div></section>

  <section class="bw-section" id="finishes" aria-labelledby="finish-title"><div class="bw-section-head"><div><p class="bw-kicker">03 / PROCESS IS PART OF THE DESIGN</p><h2 id="finish-title">Same motif.<br/><em>Different object.</em></h2></div><p>Printing, enamel and embroidery translate an image in different ways. These comparisons guide a quote conversation; a supplier must confirm its tolerances and process for the actual design.</p></div><div class="bw-process-grid">{studio.finishes.map(finish=><article class="bw-process"><p class="bw-kicker">{finish.feel}</p><h3>{finish.name}</h3><dl><dt>Good starting point</dt><dd>{finish.best}</dd><dt>Design tradeoff</dt><dd>{finish.tradeoff}</dd><dt>Proof to request</dt><dd>{finish.proof}</dd><dt>Material afterlife</dt><dd>{finish.endOfLife}</dd></dl></article>)}</div></section>

  <section class="bw-section bw-brief-section" id="brief" aria-labelledby="brief-title"><div class="bw-section-head"><div><p class="bw-kicker">04 / TAKE A BETTER QUESTION TO A MAKER</p><h2 id="brief-title" tabindex="-1">Your idea.<br/><em>A useful brief.</em></h2></div><p>Choose a visual study and a process. Add your intended use and the question you want to answer. Download a plain-text discussion brief, including your current pilot assumptions.</p></div><p data-interactive-note class="bw-note">Interactive tools require JavaScript. The concept details and blank workbook below remain readable.</p><div class="bw-tools"><form class="bw-form" data-brief-form><label for="bw-concept">Concept study</label><select name="concept" id="bw-concept" disabled>{studio.concepts.map(c=><option value={c.id}>{c.name} / {c.family}</option>)}</select><label for="bw-finish">Process to discuss</label><select name="finish" id="bw-finish" disabled>{studio.finishes.map(f=><option value={f.id}>{f.name}</option>)}</select><label for="bw-quote-quantity">Quantity to quote</label><input id="bw-quote-quantity" name="quantity" maxlength="20" value="25 / 50 / 100" disabled/><label for="bw-audience">Intended audience / use</label><input id="bw-audience" name="audience" maxlength="160" placeholder="Adult decorative gift; intended surface or attachment…" disabled/><label for="bw-question">What should the pilot teach you?</label><textarea id="bw-question" name="question" maxlength="600" rows="4" placeholder="Will this read clearly at 28 mm? Will the backing stay attached?" disabled></textarea><p data-compatibility class="bw-note">Plausible process to quote. The maker must confirm feasibility.</p><button class="bw-button" type="button" data-download disabled>Download my maker brief <span aria-hidden="true">↓</span></button><p role="status" aria-live="polite" aria-atomic="true" class="bw-status" data-brief-status>Nothing is submitted. Your draft is not a supplier order.</p><p class="bw-note" style="margin-top:20px"><a href="/buildworks/pilot-workbook.txt" download>Download the blank pilot workbook ↓</a></p></form><div class="bw-preview"><div class="bw-preview-head"><img data-brief-preview src="/images/buildworks/jetty-arc.svg" width="510" height="440" alt="Jetty Arc, original flat concept illustration"/><div><p class="bw-kicker">BRIEF PREVIEW</p><p><strong>Original design.<br/>Open questions.</strong></p></div></div><pre data-brief-output tabindex="0" aria-label="Maker brief preview">{makerBrief(studio.concepts[0],studio.finishes[0],{},pilot)}</pre></div></div><noscript><p class="bw-noscript">Read all six concepts and process comparisons above, or <a href="/buildworks/pilot-workbook.txt" download>download the blank workbook</a>. Brief editing and the live calculator need JavaScript; controls are disabled to prevent submission.</p></noscript></section>

  <section class="bw-section" id="business" aria-labelledby="business-title"><div class="bw-section-head"><div><p class="bw-kicker">05 / PRICE THE LEARNING, TOO</p><h2 id="business-title">A small run.<br/><em>Real questions.</em></h2></div><p>An illustrative USD model for one product, one item per direct order. Every value is editable. These are assumptions, not actual sales, supplier quotes or a demand forecast.</p></div><div class="bw-tools"><form class="bw-form" data-calculator><fieldset><legend>Model a first batch</legend><div class="bw-pair">{fields.map(field=><div><label for={`bw-${field.key}`}>{field.label}</label><input id={`bw-${field.key}`} name={field.key} type="number" inputmode={field.step==='1'?'numeric':'decimal'} min={INPUT_LIMITS[field.key][0]} max={INPUT_LIMITS[field.key][1]} step={field.step} value={PILOT_DEFAULTS[field.key]} required disabled/></div>)}</div></fieldset><button class="bw-button bw-button--light" type="button" disabled data-reset>Reset illustrative assumptions</button><p class="bw-error" role="status" aria-live="polite" data-calc-error></p><p class="bw-status" role="status" aria-live="polite" aria-atomic="true" data-calc-status></p></form><div class="bw-results" data-results aria-label="Illustrative pilot results"><h3>What this batch asks of you.</h3><div class="bw-result-grid"><div class="bw-result"><span>UPFRONT BATCH CASH</span><strong data-result="cash">{money(pilot.cashRequired)}</strong><span data-result="units">{pilot.saleable} saleable / {pilot.rejected} rejected</span></div><div class="bw-result"><span>LANDED / SALEABLE UNIT</span><strong data-result="landed">{money(pilot.landed)}</strong><span data-result="gross">{pilot.grossMargin.toFixed(1)}% gross margin</span></div><div class="bw-result"><span>DIRECT CONTRIBUTION / UNIT</span><strong data-result="contribution">{money(pilot.contribution)}</strong><span data-result="net">{pilot.contributionMargin.toFixed(1)}% after selling costs</span></div><div class="bw-result"><span>BATCH CASH RESULT</span><strong data-result="cashResult">{money(pilot.pilotCashResult)}</strong><span data-result="sold">{pilot.sold} assumed sold / {pilot.unsold} unsold</span></div></div><ul><li data-result="breakEven">{pilot.toolingBreakEven} units to recover tooling</li><li data-result="recovery">{pilot.cashRecoveryUnits} sales to recover all batch cash</li><li data-result="wholesale">{pilot.wholesaleGrossMargin.toFixed(1)}% wholesale gross margin</li></ul><p>Gross margin compares sale price with landed cost. Contribution also subtracts seller-paid shipping, fees and the return reserve. Neither is net business profit.</p><p>Batch cash result charges the entire batch upfront, including unsold inventory, plus tooling/samples. Sell-through rounds down to whole units; rejects reduce saleable stock.</p><p class="bw-note">Wholesale shows gross margin only. Add freight, payment terms, discounts, returns and channel costs before accepting an order.</p></div></div><details class="bw-assumptions"><summary>Read the math and what is missing</summary><p>Saleable units = floor(ordered × (1 − reject %)). Landed cost = (ordered × (unit cost + packaging) + inbound freight) ÷ saleable units. Packaging is assumed purchased for every ordered unit.</p><p>Net receipt per sale = price − percentage fee − fixed fee − seller-paid outbound shipping − return reserve. Contribution = net receipt − landed cost. Tooling break-even = ceil(tooling ÷ positive contribution). Cash recovery units = ceil(all batch cash ÷ positive net receipt); they can exceed available stock.</p><p>Batch cash result = floor(saleable × sell-through %) × net receipt − (all inventory cash + tooling). Unsold stock is still an asset with uncertain recoverable value; this conservative cash view expenses its full cost immediately.</p><p>This model excludes taxes, import duties, labor, overhead, insurance, storage, financing, discounts and multi-item order effects. The return reserve is an expected expense allowance, not a return-rate prediction. Shipping subsidies must be included honestly. Negative contributions have no tooling break-even.</p><p>Start with 30%, 70% and 100% sell-through as arbitrary sensitivity scenarios; do not treat them as demand evidence. Replace every assumption with actual quotes and observed pilot data before a production decision.</p></details></section>

  <section class="bw-section" aria-labelledby="models-title"><div class="bw-section-head"><div><p class="bw-kicker">06 / THREE WAYS TO START SMALL</p><h2 id="models-title">A business plan<br/><em>with gates.</em></h2></div><p>Keep the product, operating cost and cash cycle visible. A storefront, a local retailer and a commission each create different commitments.</p></div><div class="bw-business"><article><h3>Direct pilot</h3><p>One design, one process, a modest quantity. Test interest with interviews or clearly labeled mockups before committing cash.</p><ul><li>Budget tooling, samples, inventory, rejected pieces and pack-out labor.</li><li>Pay deposits/balance on agreed terms; stock and freight cash leave before sales arrive.</li><li>Record conversion, damage, return reasons and support time; these are future observations.</li><li>Set an honest dispatch window, return/refund terms and lost/damaged parcel policy before selling.</li></ul></article><article><h3>Wholesale capsule</h3><p>A few coordinated objects for an independent retailer. Price for the actual channel rather than assuming retail margin survives a discount.</p><ul><li>Quote case packs, minimums, samples, packaging and retailer requirements.</li><li>Model payment delay, retailer margin, returns, markdown support and outbound cartons.</li><li>A purchase order has obligations; consignment leaves stock risk with the studio.</li><li>Reorder only from sell-through evidence and verified capacity/lead time.</li></ul></article><article><h3>Custom commission</h3><p>A visual motif for a venue, event or community group, subject to rights permission and a written project scope.</p><ul><li>Separate design fee, prototype/tooling, unit manufacture, freight and rush charges.</li><li>Define licensed artwork use, revisions, approvals, cancellation and defect/remake terms.</li><li>Schedule around sample approval and production windows; reserve time for a failed proof.</li><li>No venue, city, school or brand collaboration is claimed by these concept studies.</li></ul></article></div></section>

  <section class="bw-section" id="quality" aria-labelledby="quality-title"><div class="bw-section-head"><div><p class="bw-kicker">07 / CARE BELONGS IN THE BRIEF</p><h2 id="quality-title">Proof it.<br/><em>Then prove it.</em></h2></div><p>A digital proof is an artwork check. A physical sample checks materials and construction. Neither alone certifies product safety, durability or a complete production run.</p></div><div class="bw-qc"><div><h3>The first-pilot quality record</h3><ol><li><strong>Freeze the specification.</strong> Record artwork revision, dimensions, color references, substrate, thickness, finish, attachments and packing method. Use supplier-specific minimum line and bleed guidance.</li><li><strong>Approve the sample separately.</strong> Compare the physical sample with the marked proof in controlled light. Agree measurable tolerances and acceptable color/material variation in writing.</li><li><strong>Inspect the first small lot.</strong> Proposed pilot policy: inspect every unit, photograph defects and count pack-out. Check print/finish, edges, attachments and function against the retained sample.</li><li><strong>Test the real journey.</strong> Handling, pin/clutch retention, backing adhesion, cord/holes, abrasion and a packaged shipping trial need product-specific procedures. Do not invent a load or durability rating.</li><li><strong>Record a release decision.</strong> Retain lot identification and evidence. For later larger batches, agree sampling and critical/major/minor defect rules with a qualified quality professional; no AQL certification is claimed here.</li></ol></div><div><h3>Rights, age, materials and waste</h3><ul><li>Keep a rights ledger for drawings, photographs, fonts and marks. A city seal, team emblem or commercial character needs permission or a valid applicable license. Original art does not establish trademark clearance.</li><li>Determine intended age and use before selecting labels. Small parts rules, sharp pin points, magnet ingestion hazards and children’s-product testing/traceability can apply. An “adult collectible” statement is not a substitute for classification.</li><li>Ask for exact alloy, inks, coatings, adhesive and backing composition plus relevant test reports. Report scope must match the material, age category and product; “non-toxic” or “child-safe” is not established by a supplier slogan.</li><li>Start with a simple backing card or paper band where it protects the piece. Compare damaged-goods waste with packaging saved; avoid unnecessary laminate and mixed-material decoration.</li><li>Verify recycled content, sourcing and disposal claims for the actual item and local collection system. Keep care instructions and batch identifiers legible. Supplier certification claims are leads to verify.</li></ul><p class="bw-note">US rules and source links below provide starting points. Product-specific compliance and destination-market requirements need qualified review before sale.</p></div></div></section>

  <section class="bw-section bw-research" id="sources" aria-labelledby="sources-title"><div class="bw-section-head"><div><p class="bw-kicker">08 / OPEN SOURCE NOTEBOOK · CHECKED {studio.date}</p><h2 id="sources-title">Read the<br/><em>fine print.</em></h2></div><p>Primary sources and public supplier examples. Minimums, prices and lead times can change. These are research leads, with no affiliate links, quote acceptance or endorsement.</p></div><ol class="bw-sources">{studio.sources.map(source=><li class="bw-source"><p class="bw-kicker">{source.category} / {source.checked}</p><h3><a href={source.url}>{source.title} ↗</a></h3><p>{source.summary}</p><p class="bw-note"><strong>Limits.</strong> {source.limit}</p></li>)}</ol><p class="bw-note" style="margin-top:25px">Prices are in USD unless stated. Use the exact source configuration; do not scale a price to a different size, finish or quantity. Compare total landed cost, sample approvals, defect terms and delivery window. No supplier has been contacted.</p></section>

  <section class="bw-section" id="school" aria-labelledby="school-title"><div class="bw-section-head"><div><p class="bw-kicker">09 / BUSINESS × DESIGN / AN OPEN CASE</p><h2 id="school-title">Make the object.<br/><em>Keep the lesson.</em></h2></div><p>A proposed Buildworks case for University of El Segundo business/design study and IndustryNext. Anyone can use these exercises; no class enrollment, student project, institutional agreement or partner engagement is implied.</p></div><div class="bw-lesson-grid"><article><p class="bw-kicker">DESIGN EXERCISE / 45 MIN</p><h3>Translate one motif.</h3><p>Draw one local observation in two colors. Compare enamel, print and thread at actual size. Deliver a rights log, one full-scale paper model and a list of process compromises.</p></article><article><p class="bw-kicker">BUSINESS EXERCISE / 60 MIN</p><h3>Stress the batch.</h3><p>Use the calculator for low, middle and high sell-through. Vary shipping and rejects. Deliver a cash timeline, a quote comparison and a maximum amount you can afford to lose learning.</p></article><article><p class="bw-kicker">OPERATIONS EXERCISE / 45 MIN</p><h3>Write the stop rule.</h3><p>Define sample approvals and three defect categories. Map packaging and returns. Deliver a batch record and a decision rule for repeat, revise or stop; leave untested claims blank.</p></article></div><div class="bw-links">{studio.connections.map(link=><div class="bw-link"><a href={link.href}>{link.title} ↗</a><p>{link.summary}</p></div>)}</div><aside class="bw-pending" aria-label="Proposed project connections"><p class="bw-kicker">CONNECTIONS IN PREPARATION</p>{studio.pendingConnections.map(link=><p><strong>{link.title}.</strong> {link.summary}</p>)}</aside></section>
  <footer class="bw-footer"><p>EL SEGUNDO BUILDWORKS<br/>Original PointCast studio concept / October 2026<br/>Drawn by Codex for PointCast; no brand or city endorsement.</p><nav aria-label="Buildworks downloads"><a href="/buildworks.json">Notebook JSON ↗</a><a href="/buildworks/pilot-workbook.txt" download>Pilot workbook ↓</a><a href="/">PointCast ↗</a></nav></footer>
</div>
</BlockLayout>
<script>
import studio from '../data/buildworks.json';
import {initBuildworks} from '../scripts/buildworks.mjs';
const setup=()=>initBuildworks(document.querySelector('[data-buildworks]'),studio);
setup();document.addEventListener('astro:page-load',setup);
</script>
<style is:global>body:has(.bw){background:#efe9d4}html:has(.bw){scroll-behavior:smooth}@media(prefers-reduced-motion:reduce){html:has(.bw){scroll-behavior:auto}}</style>
